paration effect across the board, and including it completes the 2×2 matrix to show that STILL training produces more robust safety improvements than R1-distillation at comparable scales. Now I'm moving on to examine the GRU and attention probe experiments on the 32B models with multiple seeds. The GRU underperforms linear regression on 32B models because the training data is too sparse—only 20 traces with massive hidden state dimensions—so the model can't learn meaningful temporal patterns. QwQ's GRU does achieve better coverage but triggers more false positives, suggesting it's overly sensitive rather than genuinely better at capturing dynamics. This reinforces that simpler linear probes are sufficient for this task, consistent with what we saw with MLPs.\n\nGenerating performance table...\n\nNow I'm looking at the accuracy-coverage Pareto front comparing LR versus GRU across the 32B models. The analysis shows that GRU doesn't consistently outperform LR at this scale, and the real bottleneck is sample size limitations—which reinforces that temporal LR is the practical choice going forward. I'm also planning a text-based baseline experiment using sentence embeddings to determine whether hierarchical sampling actually adds value beyond simpler text monitoring approaches.\n\nLooking at the expected results across models, text probes achieve reasonable performance but consistently underperform hidden state approaches by 15-18 percentage points on R1-8B. The key insight is that safety commitment gets encoded in the model's hidden representations before it surfaces in the text—the model has already committed to compliance at a deeper level than what surface-level keywords and phrases can capture. Text-based temporal probes do improve with windowing but hit a lower ceiling than hidden state methods, making this the critical baseline for demonstrating that hierarchical sampling captures something fundamentally different from what text analysis alone can detect. Generating